11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L19",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 1:30 p.m. through late afternoon","place":"The eastern and central Shiloh battlefield: Stuart’s ravine, MacArthur’s position, Bell Field, the peach orchard, and the Hamburg–Savannah Road, Tennessee","result":"Johnston concentrated Statham, Bowen, and Jackson against MacArthur and Hurlbut at about 1:30 p.m. Stuart’s isolated brigade withdrew through a ravine under fire, exposing MacArthur’s flank; MacArthur then retreated. Additional Confederate brigades attacked Prentiss while Johnston personally led Statham forward. Hurlbut abandoned the peach orchard but formed another line immediately north of it. Johnston died at about 2:30 p.m.; Beauregard assumed army command from the rear and Bragg took direct control along the Hamburg–Savannah Road. The United States line continued to hold temporarily, while renewed fighting also pushed Sherman toward another withdrawal on the west","facts":[{"label":"Stuart’s remaining force","value":"About 800 men, isolated and nearly out of ammunition"},{"label":"Stuart’s obstacle","value":"Ravine roughly 100 feet deep and 100 feet wide behind the line"},{"label":"Johnston’s fresh brigades","value":"Statham: six regiments; Bowen: four regiments"},{"label":"Attack began","value":"About 1:30 p.m."},{"label":"United States defenders","value":"MacArthur’s three Illinois regiments plus Hurlbut’s two brigades"},{"label":"Peach orchard lost","value":"Mid-afternoon after a coordinated multi-brigade assault"},{"label":"Johnston died","value":"About 2:30 p.m."},{"label":"Command succession","value":"P.G.T. Beauregard assumed command; Braxton Bragg directed the eastern fighting"}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map identifies the Hamburg–Savannah Road, Bell Field, the peach orchard, the Hornet’s Nest area, and the eastern ravines.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"United States left before the breakthrough","caption":"Stuart and MacArthur held east of Hurlbut, Prentiss, and Wallace. Johnston redirected Jackson from Stuart and combined it with Bowen and Statham for the early-afternoon attack.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-hornets-nest-noon.svg","alt":"Map of the United States left and Confederate approaches at Shiloh"},{"title":"The attack through Bell Field","caption":"Statham and Bowen advanced across Bell Field. Johnston personally moved Statham forward, Hurlbut left the peach orchard, and Johnston was fatally wounded after the position fell.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/death-of-albert-sidney-johnston-tour-stop-17.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-johnston-last-charge.svg","alt":"Map of the Confederate attack through Bell Field and Johnston’s fatal wounding"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the fields, roads, camps, ravines, and army positions across Shiloh.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"4:46","audioTime":286,"title":"The western and central fronts were temporarily stable","detail":"Sherman and McClernand paused near Jones Field, while Wallace and Prentiss had repulsed attacks at the Hornet’s Nest."},{"time":"6:35","audioTime":395,"title":"Stuart remained isolated on the far left","detail":"About 800 men had no artillery, little ammunition, a deep ravine behind them, and no direct contact with MacArthur."},{"time":"8:17","audioTime":497,"title":"Johnston assembled fresh brigades","detail":"Statham’s six regiments deployed west of the Hamburg–Savannah Road and Bowen’s four regiments east of it; Jackson was redirected to support Bowen."},{"time":"9:55","audioTime":595,"title":"The attack opened at about 1:30 p.m.","detail":"Bowen and Jackson advanced against MacArthur while Statham moved toward Hurlbut in the peach orchard."},{"time":"10:28","audioTime":628,"title":"Johnston moved Bowen forward","detail":"When Bowen stopped at a shallow ravine under heavy fire, Johnston rode along the line and urged the men to resume their advance."},{"time":"12:36","audioTime":756,"title":"Hurlbut and MacArthur ran low on ammunition","detail":"The 31st Illinois was ordered to stop firing and prepare to use bayonets; Stuart’s men were searching casualties for cartridges."},{"time":"13:15","audioTime":795,"title":"Stuart ordered a withdrawal","detail":"Confederates fired down the ravine and from its rim, turning the attempted orderly movement into a rout."},{"time":"14:16","audioTime":856,"title":"MacArthur’s flank was exposed","detail":"Jackson struck the 50th Illinois from the side while Bowen pressed from the front. The 50th, 9th, and 12th Illinois withdrew in succession."},{"time":"14:51","audioTime":891,"title":"Hurlbut shifted troops toward his open left","detail":"With Stuart and MacArthur gone, Hurlbut reinforced the threatened end of his line and continued defending the peach orchard."},{"time":"15:22","audioTime":922,"title":"More Confederate brigades entered the attack","detail":"Zachariah Deas led Gladden’s former brigade and George Maney led Stephens’s former brigade against Prentiss."},{"time":"16:12","audioTime":972,"title":"The 45th Tennessee refused another advance","detail":"The regiment had been stopped by the 41st Illinois. Breckinridge and Tennessee governor Isham Harris could not immediately get it moving."},{"time":"17:36","audioTime":1056,"title":"Johnston personally led Statham forward","detail":"His intervention produced a coordinated multi-brigade attack that continued through heavy infantry and artillery fire."},{"time":"18:29","audioTime":1109,"title":"Hurlbut abandoned the peach orchard","detail":"The United States infantry withdrew to the woods north of the orchard and stopped the Confederates again with heavy fire."},{"time":"19:09","audioTime":1149,"title":"Johnston died as the advance paused","detail":"At about 2:30 p.m., Johnston died in a ravine south of Bell Field after bleeding from the leg wound described in episode 117."},{"time":"20:08","audioTime":1208,"title":"Beauregard assumed army command","detail":"Isham Harris delivered the news. Beauregard remained at the rear headquarters, while Bragg moved to direct the fighting along the Hamburg–Savannah Road."},{"time":"21:21","audioTime":1281,"title":"The Confederate left renewed its attack","detail":"Cleburne’s damaged brigade and Preston Pond’s nearly fresh Louisiana brigade attacked Sherman and McClernand, prompting another planned withdrawal."},{"time":"22:41","audioTime":1361,"title":"Pressure shifted toward the center","detail":"Patton Anderson attacked across Duncan Field. The assault failed but began the final sequence against the Hornet’s Nest."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Moved along the front, encouraged division commanders, and continued expecting Lew Wallace’s arrival"},{"commander":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Two brigades in the peach orchard; shifted troops left and formed a new line north of it"},{"commander":"John McArthur","command":"Detached brigade, Wallace’s 2nd Division","subordinates":"9th, 12th, and 50th Illinois between Hurlbut and Stuart"},{"commander":"David Stuart","command":"Detached brigade, Sherman’s 5th Division","subordinates":"About 800 men from the 54th Ohio, 55th Illinois, and part of the 71st Ohio"},{"commander":"Benjamin M. Prentiss / W. H. L. Wallace","command":"United States center","subordinates":"Held while Deas, Maney, and later Anderson attacked"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman / John A. McClernand","command":"United States right","subordinates":"Held near Jones Field before renewed mid-afternoon attacks"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Albert Sidney Johnston","command":"Army of the Mississippi until about 2:30 p.m.","subordinates":"Organized and personally led the eastern attack; killed after the peach orchard fell"},{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Mississippi after Johnston’s death","subordinates":"Continued directing the battle from the rear"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg","command":"Eastern battlefield after Johnston’s death","subordinates":"Renewed pressure against Hurlbut along the Hamburg–Savannah Road"},{"commander":"John C. Breckinridge","command":"Reserve Corps","subordinates":"Statham’s and Bowen’s brigades in Johnston’s main assault"},{"commander":"William S. Statham / John S. Bowen","command":"Fresh brigades","subordinates":"Ten regiments attacking Hurlbut and MacArthur"},{"commander":"John K. Jackson / James R. Chalmers","command":"Brigades on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Jackson turned toward MacArthur; Chalmers pursued Stuart through the ravine"},{"commander":"Zachariah C. Deas / George Maney","command":"Brigades attacking Prentiss","subordinates":"Succeeded earlier wounded brigade commanders"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Albert Sidney Johnston","role":"Organized and personally led the successful attack on the United States left, then died from his wound","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/albert-sidney-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670318/"},{"name":"P.G.T. Beauregard","role":"Succeeded Johnston as commander of the Army of the Mississippi and remained at the rear headquarters","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Took direct control of the eastern fighting after Johnston’s death","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670042/"},{"name":"John C. Breckinridge","role":"Deployed Statham and Bowen and attempted to restart stalled regiments","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/john-c-breckinridge.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666692/"},{"name":"Isham G. Harris","role":"Helped rally Tennessee troops and carried news of Johnston’s death to Beauregard","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","role":"Held the peach orchard, shifted troops toward his exposed flank, and formed another line after withdrawing","side":"United States"},{"name":"David Stuart","role":"Ordered his isolated, ammunition-starved brigade through the ravine and rallied survivors farther north","side":"United States"},{"name":"John McArthur","role":"Withdrew after attacks from Bowen and Jackson struck his front and exposed flank","side":"United States"},{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Held the western battlefield and prepared another withdrawal when attacks resumed","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670507/"}],"losses":[{"name":"General Albert Sidney Johnston","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, Army of the Mississippi","outcome":"Died from blood loss at about 2:30 p.m. after being wounded behind the right knee during the attack on Hurlbut"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"Ravines and dense woods made orderly withdrawal difficult and allowed attackers to fire into exposed flanks.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"The coordinated early-afternoon attack forced the United States left away from the peach orchard.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"},{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"Grant’s divisions continued withdrawing toward the river while he waited for Lew Wallace and Nelson.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 120","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/120-shiloh-part-the-tenth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Johnston death site","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/death-of-albert-sidney-johnston-tour-stop-17.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"}]}}]}]
